A multi-column box of auto height sizes its columns to hold where its
content’s boxes and lines reach in Ladybird, never less, even where the
content ends with a negative margin. Chrome 158 and Firefox 151 agree on
every case in the test, among them a margin collapsing through its
parent, an empty last child, lines overflowing a fixed-height box,
overlapping boxes, column-fill:auto, two columns, and a definite height
(which still overflows into a new column). WebKit 26.5 agrees on all but
two auto-filled columns, which it splits evenly. All three agree that
lines overflowing a box that avoids breaks count too, whether the box
is a child of the column root, nested deeper, or holds the lines in a
block of its own, and next to a positioned child of the box. Before a
forced break inside such a box, Chrome and WebKit agree with Ladybird
at 49px, where Firefox adds the 5px box after the break.

Two neighboring cases split the engines, and Ladybird now counts the
overflowing lines in both. In two columns, Chrome and Firefox move a
box that avoids breaks, with lines far past its end, to the second
column and grow the columns to hold them; WebKit keeps it in the first
column and grows that one, as Ladybird now does. And Firefox and WebKit
count lines overflowing a box with size containment, where Chrome
doesn’t.

In Blink, ColumnLayoutAlgorithm::CalculateBalancedColumnBlockSize()
takes each content run as the larger of BlockSizeForFragmentation() and
the fragment’s block size. Gecko’s nsColumnSetFrame measures each
column with nsLayoutUtils::CalculateContentBEnd(): the furthest block
end of a frame, its lines, and its children. And WebKit stretches the
columns by the smallest space shortage, in RenderMultiColumnSet::
calculateBalancedHeight(), while there are more of them than asked for.
